# Your own Caddy config goes in caddy.d/, not in this file. This file is
# tracked: an edit here makes the next `git pull` refuse to update, and
# `git reset --hard` undoes it without a word. caddy.d/ is ignored by git
# and mounted read-only into the caddy container, and Caddy reads two kinds
# of file from it:
#
#   caddy.d/*.caddy  blocks of their own, read here at the top level, such
#                    as a second site that redirects to this one
#   caddy.d/*.site   directives for the Patchwork site, read inside its
#                    block below, such as an access log
#
# The *.example files beside them are tracked recipes: copy one without the
# .example suffix to use it. A pattern that matches no file only logs a
# warning. A global options block works in the *.caddy file that sorts
# first, so name it something like 00-global.caddy.
#
# After changing anything in caddy.d/, reload Caddy:
#   docker compose exec -w /etc/caddy caddy caddy reload
# See docs/DEPLOYMENT.md, "Your own Caddy config".
import caddy.d/*.caddy

{$DOMAIN:localhost} {
	# Caddy's reverse_proxy ignores client-supplied X-Forwarded-For by
	# default and sets it from the connecting address itself; only
	# `trusted_proxies` opts into believing an incoming value. If another
	# proxy sits in front of Caddy, `server.trusted_proxies` in
	# patchwork.yaml is the setting that matters.
	reverse_proxy patchwork:8080

	# No access log ships, and the shipped privacy policy says so. The
	# recipe for one is caddy.d/access-log.site.example.
	import caddy.d/*.site
}

# Patchwork answers on one hostname. If you want www to work too, point a www
# record at this server and uncomment this, in that order: Caddy can only get
# a certificate for a name that already resolves, and until the record exists
# it retries a doomed order for as long as the server runs. A www record
# without this block does not help: Caddy has no certificate for the name and
# refuses the TLS handshake, so the browser trades an unknown-host error for a
# connection error.
#
# Redirect rather than serve the app on a second name. Session cookies are
# host-only and every emailed link is built from instance.domain, so an
# instance answering on both would sign someone in on one name and mail them
# links to the other.
#
# www.{$DOMAIN:localhost} {
# 	redir https://{$DOMAIN:localhost}{uri} permanent
# }
